# THE PUBLISHED KERNEL IMAGE this checkout runs when it is run as a container (si#200), and the file
# si#201's launcher reads.
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT A MANIFEST KEY. The launcher is bash. It has to know the image reference BEFORE any
# Python exists to parse simplon.yaml with - that is the whole premise of the second route - so the pin
# is a plain line a shell can read:
#
#     ref="$(grep -v '^[[:space:]]*#' deploy/image/image.pin | grep -v '^[[:space:]]*$' | head -n1)"
#
# WHAT THE LINE IS. One image reference, pinned to a version. Never ':latest': a launcher that pulled a
# moving tag would run a different kernel tomorrow against the same checkout, and `docker.pinned_image`
# refuses the shape for that reason everywhere else in this repository. tests/test_own_image.py holds
# both halves - that the line is pinned, and that its registry and repository are the ones simplon.yaml
# declares under `images: kernel:` - so the pin cannot drift from the image the release actually builds.
#
# WHEN IT MOVES. In the commit that publishes a new image. `release:image` is on demand rather than on
# every release (simplon.yaml says why), so publishing and pinning are already one person's single act.
#
# THERE IS NO LINE YET, and that is a statement rather than an omission: no simplon image has been
# published at the time of writing. si#200 was proven end to end against a local `registry:2` - built,
# pushed, read back, purged locally, pulled by version and run - and nothing has gone to a public
# registry since, so writing a reference here would be a claim nobody has checked. A launcher that finds
# no line must say exactly that: this checkout has no published kernel image, so the container route is
# not available and the venv route is.
#
# WHAT CHANGED IN si#313 IS WHY THERE IS NO LINE. It used to be that there was no route to publish at
# all: the registry was Docker Hub, which needs a stored credential this repository deliberately does not
# keep. The registry is `ghcr.io/marcozwyssig` now and needs none, so the only thing between here and a
# line is somebody running `./simplon.sh release image` on a tagged checkout.
#
# AND THE ORDER IS PUBLISH FIRST, PIN AFTERWARDS (si#313, decided). `release:image` reads the tag back out
# of the registry before it reports OK, so a pin written after it names something that demonstrably
# exists. Written the other way round, this file would name an image that is not there yet - and on a
# machine with no python3 the launcher would then take the container route and fail, where today it says
# plainly that no image is pinned.
